There are new rules for non-EU packages, customers often don't deliver data on them
Lukáš Neuheisl also orders from abroad several times a month.
He buys mainly collectible cards.
“Usually it can be tens of dollars, let’s say ten dollars higher, where it is still worth importing, especially from Japan, where the post office is often free of charge.”
Starting in October, the ordering of small consignments has been slightly more expensive, it must newly add VAT and the post office have to hand over data for the customs procedure.
An e-mail arrives to him, stating that customs officers are awaiting the arrival of the package.
Then it is enough to fill in the details of the consignment, and if the trader did not include VAT already during the sale, it will be assessed by the customs office from the total amount for the shipment and for the transport.
In the event that the addresseee does not seize the customs procedure himself/herself, remuneration must also be added to the total amount.
According to Lukas Neuheisel, however, the whole process is not complicated.
I'm going to click one or two tipping boxes, put in two attachments and I'm done.
For me it's usually a matter of five minutes, Neuheisl says.
However, not all shipments can be delivered smoothly.
Due to the new customs rules, the daily number of received shipments from abroad has fallen from 60 thousand to 15 thousand.
In addition, according to the Czech Post, the problem is that people do not respond to calls for the supply of data that are needed to complete the customs procedure.
There are currently 30 thousand shipments at the international post office, which we have to process.
If people filled in all the data that are needed and would be filled in on time, we would have been here about a halved, Czech Post spokeswoman Matyáš Vitík said.
