The trains are starting to run according to the new timetable, the carriers will change somewhere
Starting from Sunday, trains begin to run on the railway according to the new timetable.
The biggest change is the replacement of hauliers on some lines, for example between Ústí nad Labem and Kolín, where RegioJet begins to run instead of Czech Railways.
For most of the lines, it only adjusts the time of departure, or slightly their route.
There will also be dozens of new trains.
Carriers started selling tickets already during the autumn.
Czech Railways is planning an average of 6,6783 passenger transport services per day in the new timetable, of which there will average 478 long-distance long-distance trains per day.
The trains will travel approximately 118 million kilometers during the new timetable.
In addition to domestic connections under the new timetable, the railways will also go to Germany, Poland, Slovakia, Hungary, Austria and Switzerland.
Together with the new timetable, the company will deploy dozens of new trains.
The main novelty will be InterJet trains, which will run on lines from Prague to Cheb.
Other new trains of the carrier will be transported in northern Moravia or in western Bohemia.
As of next year, the carrier will traditionally increase faring prices by an average of 3.2 per cent.
Railways takes into account inflation into their tariffs every year.
The biggest change in the RegioJet timetable is the entrance to R23 line Ústí nad Labem – Mělník – Nymburk – Kolín.
After the success in the competition of the Ministry of Transport, the carrier will be replaced by Czech Railways.
Every day RegioJet will dispatch a total of 16 connections on the track, eight in each direction.
Further changes relate to long-distance connections between Prague and Brno, which from Sunday will also halt in stations Havlíčkův Brod, Žďár nad Sázavou and Kolín.
Leo Express retained its 16 connections, two return connections to Slovakia, as well as weekend connections to Kraków.
According to spokeswoman Emil Sedlařík, the carrier, despite the planned lock-out works, has also tried to preserve the most similar driving times of its long-distance trains.
Without major changes, the operation of Arriva trains and other hauliers should also continue.
The haulier will also be exchanged in some regional lines.
Changes await passengers, for example, in the Česká Lípa region, where on the line from Mladá Boleslav via Česká Lípa to Rumburk will be operated by Trilex by the German company Die Länderbahn.
Passengers will also be able to use unified railway fares for the second consecutive year.
As with Czech Railways, their price will increase by an inflation rate of 3.2 per cent.
