TVs have fallen for the trend of Christmas movies, two hundred of them premiere this year
Los Angeles (Reuters) – Cinemas, TV stations and streaming platforms in the United States and other English-speaking countries have fallen for the trend of Christmas movies, and this year it will present a record more than two-hundred to their viewers.
This was calculated by the IMDb film database operator.
The genre of Christmas family and romantic films in recent years has scoring and significantly increasing the viewership, so these films are being created more and more.
There have been four times more Christmas movies this year than in 2011 and twice as many as five years ago.
The IMDb database included only those films that have the word "Christmas) in its title, so there will actually be much more festive films.
There have always been films that people traditionally associate with Christmas.
Fairy fairy tales are mainly associated with this period in the Czech Republic, for example, films Alone at Home are also popular worldwide, the Love of Heaven or the classic Christmas story Life is beautiful from 1946.
However, the true boom of the Christmas films did not start until 2009, when an U.S. cable television station Hallmark came up with a special film franchise, the BBC recalled.
Her Advent project called Countdown to Christmas included four films at that time and it was very successful.
Earlier this year, the station started tuning its viewers at Christmas on October 22 and will present a total of 42 Christmas films.
Competent Lifetime has 35 new Christmas-team movies on the agenda this year, and popular streaming platforms such as Netflix contribute to the sum.
“In this magical period, it doesn’t really matter so much, the important thing is that there are a lot of christmas trees in the background and that it is snowing,” said Brandon Gray, the author of a book about Christmas films called I’ll Be Home for Christmas Movies.
“It’s a form of escaping for viewers and a way to feel a little peace in the middle of all the festive frenzy and the madness of the world we’ve been living in for at least two hours.”
According to him, for example, Hallmark still uses the same recipe for its films, which is uniform but successful.
You've got two people who fall in love with each other, but then it comes about half an hour before the end some misunderstood, which, of course, will be successfully resolved, and the two of them polish.
That's how it is over and over again, and as long as all the movies look the same and have a similar vibrancy, people are watching each other, Gray adds.
