Defending of the system
When an important Czech lawyer and constitutional judiciary Vojtěch Cepl in 1999 responded to a journalistic question of what the Czech Constitution meant to him – whether the sanctuary, on which he swears and which he learns in the school since childhood, or, conversely, an agreement that can be rescheduled if necessary, he definitely inclined towards the first conception.
We once agree in a constitution on the democratic rules of our lives, which at the same time define who we as the state and its citizens are, and it is easier to spare with the changes.
Let’s imagine: some nations even love their rules.
Just as the Czechs like dumpling with pork and cabbage, Vojtěch Cepl glossed at that time.
Lately, however, there is an increasing opinion among lawyers that the Constitution of the Czech Republic needs changes.
For the years it has been tested by situations that its creators (to whom Vojtěch Cepl belonged) could not foresee, for example with the behavior of the directly elected president.
In one thing Cepl was right.
Everything we know about such documents shows that political interference with their texts must be thoughtful.
The Constitution must be understood and actively defended, only then it can be the key to combing most of the crises that societies encounter throughout history.
Among other things, the Constitution is a kind of order of governance consisting of the individual rules, which set the boundaries of the game for politicians.
We are afraid that power will be misused against minorities or individuals, so we bind politicians with prohibitions.
At the same time, the constitutional texts also make it possible for politicians to have their power.
