His hip, or was he his hip?
At first glance, it is nothing complicated.
The majority of nouns in Czech express only one grammatical genus, and it is therefore not a problem to determine whether it is a masculine, feminine, or high-medium genus.
But then there is also a relatively large group of nouns, for which the genus is not settled.
Such substantive names fluctuate between two feathers.
During inclusation, they then acquire double endings and, in some cases, remain in an inflexible form.
For example, the words “strength” or “skier” are masculine and feminine, in the for the first one they are inclined according to the “machine pattern”, in the second according to “song”.
For the next group of nouns, there are different shapes already in the first fall of the singular, for example: "line/line", "kedluben/kedlubna" or "brambor/brambora" (in the sense of food).
At the same time, both shapes are standard, they have the same meaning and are therefore freely interchangeable.
Some expressions may differ regionally, such as “cucumber” in the Czech Republic and “cucumbers” in Moravia, but in this case the Moravian variant is unwritten, so are other Czech-Moravian pairs of words: “movel” and “trigger”, “doughnuts” and “doughnuts”, etc.
Some of the words that have penetratedited from other languages from other languages have originally been inclinable, but they are gradually adopting Czech endings.
A typical example is the term "image", which is masculine and feminine genus, or the word "buffet", which remained uncultivated in the middle genus, but in the masculine ends according to the "castle".
