How the pandemic affected intimate life: Growing the number of people under the age of 35 who are without having sex throughout the year
Increasingly young adults in the U.S. are living their lives without having sex.
These are mainly religious people, according to the DailyMail's website.
A poll has shown that from 2008 to 2021, the proportion of people under 35 who renounce sexual life has risen from eight to 21 per cent.
Women between the age of 18 and 35, who said they did not have sexual intercourse in the last year, are more than ever before.
Other factors also contribute to the decrease in the number of sexually active individuals, a survey of the Institute for Familiar Studies (IFS).
One of them may be the economic effects of the coronavirus pandemic and a higher percentage of unemployment.
But the presence of media, social networks and video games that make sex less and a lesser-than-importer priority for young people could also contribute.
"Since 2010, the proportion of men and women between the ages of age is growing rapidly, stating that they did not have sex in the previous year," IFS researcher Lyman Stone said.
For people in marriage, sexual activity is more common, in 2021, only 5% of them said they have had sex-free over the past year.
For single people it was 29%. Stone added that marriage under the age of 35 is only a small per cent.
Fear of prenuptial interjury and religious establishment also contributes to the loss of sexual activity.
Although the spouses are more likely to be sexually announcing, the percentage of married and married under the age of 35 continues to decline.
Young people share an opinion on premarital sex, about a 30% think it is a bad thing, while about 70 per cent think it's okay.
"It's true that they're a misdemeanor among single individuals in this age-of-a-a-halv group, but their behavior shifts this trend," Stone said.
For most of those who have a moral prejurogacy problem, it is the reason for a religious establishment.
Since 2008, abstinence ratio has risen from up to less than 60 % among free people under the age of 35 who attend religious encounters more than once a month.
“Long-bearing believers”, the trend has risen from 10 to 20%,” Stone said.
Other factors also contribute to the decrease in sexual activities, such as less social interaction and, most importantly, social drinking of alcohol during the pandemic.
The study also showed the odds of sex being smaller in people out of work, or with lower-in-exerceptions.
Another reason is the expansion of digital media, which seems to reduce the need for sex.
People spend more time online, thus “replacing” this need.
This trend took hold mainly under lockdown during the coronavirus pandemic pandemic.
