The Middle East suffers from unusually dry months.
Winter is the only time of the year it is raining.
“Already the complete absence of rainfall during November, as we noted at some stations, is unusual,” the Israeli Meteorological Service said.
Kfar Giladi village in northern Israel, for example, reports just six per cent of the long-term rainfall average for November.
Two-Daiday this week's rain was therefore rather an exception.
It's good for us.
It hadn't been raining for a long time.
Also, it's the right Christmas backdrop, cheered the residents of Nazarut Wasím Aškar.
Precipitations in Israel come almost exclusively in the winter months, they are grazing and irregular.
There are forests depending on the winter rains.
Without them, they dry out and susceptible to fires.
It is not only about the forests, but also supplies of drinking water and irrigation for farmers.
The largest freshwater center in Israel, Lake Galilee, this spring, thanks to the three last rainy winters, it filled its brim with three recent rainy winters.
The level has been dropping ever since.
Water managers have been warned of drought a long time ago.
"It is to be expected that, depending on the global warming and climate changes, there may be fewer rainfall here," Uri Schor, a spokeswoman for the Israeli Water Accuracy Authority, said in 2018.
Israel can use technologies such as desalination or wastewater recycling.
There are economically disadvantaged countries, such as Lebanon, Syria and Jordan.
In the streets of the capital, Ammman, there is a growing number of tank wagons.
Idiots and private wells are drying up.
“This year my orders have risen by seventy-to-eight to eighty per cent compared to the two previous years,” Imad Suleiman said in September.
Clashes broke out in Isfahan in Isfahan between farmers and security forces.
The reason for the protests was drought.
The trough of the local river found itself completely without water.
The region has had the drizziest November in many years.
Israel prepares military raid against Iran
An Israeli defense minister has stated that the talks did not bring "nuclear progresses" in Vienna and that he had informed Washington about the preparations for an attack on Iran's nuclear facilities.
Minister Benny Gantz said on Saturday that he had ordered the Israeli army to prepare for the possibility of a military strike against Iran, Jonathan Lis reports.
Gantz, which is residing in the U.S., is trying to persuade Americans to escalculate their pressure on Iran, but it also informed Washington of military preparations.
Speaking during a press conference in Florida, Gantz said nuclear talks in Vienna had "no progress" and world powers "understood that the Iranians are messing with them."
