The next government is coming to an end and the law on the involvement of municipalities in the choice of storage nowhere
The suggestion of Minister Karel Havlíček needs to fundamentally overhaude
Prime Minister Andrej Babiš's government is over, and the law, which was supposed to ensure respect for the interests of municipalities and their citizens in selecting and authorizing a deep geological repository of highly radioactive waste, still does not exist.
The Legislative Council of the government interrupted the deliberation of a proposal submitted by the Ministry of Industry and Commerce to the government after years of delays.
Its content, however, is in a serious contradiction not only with the affected municipalities associated in the Platform Against Deep Repository, but also with the Union of Towns and Municipalities of the Czech Republic.
Self-governments await a significant reinforcement of their possibilities in decision-to-repository, which has been promised by mayors since 2011, when the first work on this legislature began, which is demanded by the Czech Atomic Act and the European directive.
We expect the new government, in accordance with its coalition treaty, to overhaute the proposal in cooperation with municipalities.
The bill from Minister Karel Havlíček, which the Platform has at its disposal, accuses municipalities in particular: The Promised Degree of Involvement of Municipalities and the Public in the decision-making process on the selection of a locality for storage is insufficient and cannot ensure respect for the interests of municipalities and their citizens.
It can only be really effective if municipalities or the public can influence whether the process will continue at all in the locality.
This can be ensured by the immovability of the Repository Administration to request the consent of the municipalities concerned prior to initiating a specific procedure.
Submitted draft substantitious intent almost completely omits the involvement of the public and makes the citizens of the municipalities practically mere statists in permitting proceedings.
The suggestion lacks a system adjustment of compensation for municipalities for the whole process of searching and selecting the location for storage, its permitting and operation.
Representations of municipalities, according to today's legal norms, do not have much opportunity to defend the interests of their citizens in the search for a place for storage.
Only in certain permitting procedures can they submit their comments or appeal, but it is decided by an office or a minister in whose interest it is possible to issue a permit.
A possible action does not have suspensive effect on carrying out exploratory or construction work.
Commencement of self-government requested by the Platform is a principle commonly used in many democratically developed countries and certainly in those where they have already progressed in permitting a repository, such as in Sweden or Finland.
In addition, the preparation of the law is another failure of the state administration, which hires external law firms to prepare the legislation.
In this case, it is a contract with HAVEL & PARTNERS s.r.o., which was concluded by SÚRAO and a follow-up to contracts with attorney Jan Zemánek.
The total amount for these works, according to the register of contracts, should amount to almost CZK 4 million.
Antonín Seknička, Vice-Mayor of the municipality of Cejle from Hrádek locality and a spokesperson for the Platform Against Deep Repository, said: After the ministers of industry, who only postponed the settlement of the status of self-government towards state authorities in the search for a deep geological repository of highly radioactive waste to their successors, we are expecting a significant turnaround from the new government.
We also offer a helping hand to do this.
For their support, we also thank the Union of Towns and Municipalities, which perceives the issue of insufficient rights of municipalities for such an essential construction similarly, as the directly affected municipalities in selected localities.
The deep-sea repository platform associates 51 members (35 municipalities and towns and 16 associations) in order to push through a change in state's approach to the management of spent nuclear fuel and other radioactive waste, which will not be confiscated only into deep geological repositories.
The platform further advocates that the decision on the selection of a locality for possible storage should be conditioned by the prior consent of the municipalities concerned.
